
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Explored the repo. It's a minimal CommonJS project: [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m exports [38;5;153mgreet[39m, [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m calls it, no dependencies
  and no test infrastructure at all.

  [1mClassification: bounded.[0m A pure predicate function added to the existing [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — the flow is already here, no
  new module or subsystem. So I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a spec.

  One thing worth deciding before I write it. Your four rules leave a gap around malformed input, and the readings
  differ in what real strings the function accepts:

  - [1mLiteral[0m — exactly your four rules, nothing more. [38;5;153ma@b@c.com[39m passes (there [3mis[0m an @, there [3mis[0m a char before it, there
    [3mis[0m a dot after). [38;5;153m"a@b.c"[39m with spaces passes. A [38;5;153mnull[39m input throws a TypeError.
  - [1mLiteral + guarded[0m — same four rules, but non-string input returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m instead of throwing. Callers can pass
    whatever they have.
  - [1mSane minimum[0m — the four rules, plus: exactly one [38;5;153m@[39m, non-empty domain, no whitespace, non-string returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m.

  What this costs you later: the first two are cheap to tighten afterward. Going the other way is not — if something
  starts relying on [38;5;153mvalidateEmail[39m and you later make it stricter, previously-accepted addresses start failing, and
  that's the change that bites. Note none of these are real RFC 5322 validation; all three will accept addresses no mail
  server would.

  I'd recommend [1mliteral + guarded[0m: it's what you asked for, and the [38;5;153mnull[39m/[38;5;153mundefined[39m guard removes the only way a caller
  can crash on it without inventing rules you didn't ask for.
